Z||000005||Phil||11-19-2000||07:52 AM||||You do know that all Fieros have the sun roof hole in the metal space frame already. Find a donor car and rob the missing parts.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||11-19-2000||08:17 AM||||KRM,<P>Phil is right, your Fiero already is prepared for a factory sunroof (VistaVent).  The hole is in the metal roof underneath the plastic skin.<P>Most aftermarket sunroofs will be much smaller that the stock one.<P>I have a full electric sunroof assembly that I got out of a donor 86 Mazda RX-7.  My daughter had one and it was a nice feature.  I got a spare for parts.  I've sold the RX-7 but still have the sunroof assembly and was thinking about what it would take to mount it in the Fiero. It doesn't seem that there's enough room for the mechanism behind the opening.<P>Do a search for sunroof and you'll see what's been posted before.  You can take the plastic roof panel off your car and get one from a sunroof donor along with the necessary hardware.<P>I believe Ed Parks may have a T-top kit.  He can be reached at:   <P>Fierofactory@Juno.com<P>or call him at (256) 420-5391<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-19-2000||01:30 PM||||The reason many aftermarket roofs won't work right/easily is the way they clamp in. the clamping setup is made for the thin sheet metat roof on most cars. The Fiero roof is several times thicker than sheet metal. You'd have to make alterations to the sunroof and that would void what ever warrantee it has.<P>A number of people here have installed factory roofs on Fiero. It's time consuming but not very dificult.||||reg||1
